WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
- To see the Earth from
space, it changes you,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.890
it changes your relationship

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.410
with this planet, with humanity.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:07.770
I really want you to come with me.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.603
Would you?

00:00:08.603 --> 00:00:09.691
- Are you serious?

00:00:09.691 --> 00:00:11.870
- I am, I think it would be meaningful.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.090
Have my brother there.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.920
- [Narrator] In this Instagram video,

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.230
Blue Origin CEO Jeff
Bezos shocked the world

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.260
when he announced that he and his brother

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.810
would be aboard the company's
first human space flight,

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.730
scheduled for July 20th.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.580
If the suborbital flight of
New Shepard is a success,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.410
it will propel Blue Origin
ahead of competitors,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.490
setting the stage for the next era

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.750
of commercial human space travel.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.910
- I wanna move out into the solar system.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.225
I want my grandchildren's grandchildren

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:41.440
to be in that world,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.920
pioneering and exploration,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.059
expansion throughout the solar system.

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:46.892
(rocket booming)

00:00:46.892 --> 00:00:47.725
- [Narrator] The launch
of New Shepard comes

00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:49.470
at a key moment for Blue Origin,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.010
which has faced setbacks
in other high-profile parts

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.040
of its business.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.930
Here's how Blue Origin blasted ahead

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:55.830
of its competition

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.130
and what its inaugural
passenger flight means

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.140
for the company.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.800
(pensive music)

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.490
In the race to launch
tourism flights into space,

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.570
Blue Origin's main
competitor, Virgin Galactic,

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:07.920
has been in the lead.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.140
- We are the first of the
commercial spaceship companies

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.480
to put people into space.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.680
- [Narrator] In May 2021,
Richard Branson's space venture

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.520
successfully launched its
third crewed space flight.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.370
- [Announcer] Three, two, one,
release, release, release.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.090
- [Narrator] A feat Blue
Origin has yet to achieve

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.480
but this is where Bezos' company hopes

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.840
to inch out ahead.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.600
The New Shepard launch in July

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.770
will have at least one paying passenger,

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.370
something Virgin Galactic has never done.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.590
- It's a key marker.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.910
- [Narrator] Doug Cameron has
covered the aerospace industry

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.390
for over 30 years.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.023
- They've won contracts,
they've won government

00:01:43.023 --> 00:01:46.080
and military deals,
they've developed rockets

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.467
but a lot of it is still
just that, in development.

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.830
So this is something which
they've actually set out

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:53.930
with an objective and achieve.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.890
- [Narrator] If everything
goes as planned,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.000
passengers will spend

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.580
a few minutes experiencing micro gravity

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:00.960
above the Karman Line,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.030
the altitude where space begins.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.140
And the cost to be on this
historic flight isn't cheap.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.900
In June, Blue Origin
held a charity auction

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.210
for a ticket to join Bezos
into suborbital space.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:16.704
- Sold, $28 million to number 107.

00:02:16.704 --> 00:02:17.537
(people clapping)
(people cheering)

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.940
- There is ultimately a perhaps

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.840
multi-billion dollar business to be built

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.260
in taking people to the edge
of space and maybe beyond.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.870
- [Narrator] But as Blue Origin plans

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.880
its first space tourism flight,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.880
larger parts of its business
have been struggling.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.520
Recently, the space
venture has faced scrutiny

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.620
for losing a couple of
high-profile government contracts.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.220
In April, SpaceX beat out Blue Origin

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.860
in a bid to build a lunar lander for NASA.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.160
NASA officials said it chose SpaceX

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.080
because its bid fit within
the agency's budget.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.230
- Blue Origin, it's pushing back on that

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.270
and it's hopeful that
more money can be found

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.180
and that it can get that kind
of big government contract,

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.020
which can help fund future vehicles

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.137
and technology going forward.

00:03:03.137 --> 00:03:04.490
- [Narrator] NASA's lunar lander

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.420
wasn't the only big-ticket deal

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.460
that Blue Origin has missed out on.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.310
In August 2020, Bezos' company lost

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.910
a multi-billion dollar Air Force contract

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.410
to SpaceX and United Launch Alliance.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.110
Industry insiders were not surprised.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.460
The Pentagon had previously indicated

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.760
that it would put a high
value on the track record

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.390
of each company in the running,

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.710
and SpaceX and United Launch Alliance

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.340
both had a longer history
of military launches

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.050
that Blue Origin did not.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:35.600
Recently, Bezos' company has
also faced development delays.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.790
Its mammoth new Glenn
rocket was originally slated

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.080
for completion this year

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.970
but has been pushed back until late 2022.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.390
- They obviously have a
number of technical hurdles

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.980
for the broader business

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.770
that they still have to cross.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.250
Every executive and engineer involved

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.610
in the space business will say

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.570
that it's hard, it's tricky.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.980
You're playing with physics here

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.920
and physics is a very hard taskmaster.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.900
- [Narrator] But aside from a rocky start,

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.950
Blue Origin's space tourism program

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.930
has run smoother than some other parts

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.020
of the business.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.950
- New Shepard's first
launch didn't go well.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.000
It blew up.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.320
They've had 15 successful ones,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.150
as well as tests of other
parts of the system since them.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:20.630
- [Narrator] And the road
to launch New Shepard

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.930
has been successful in
part due to its funding.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.960
Blue Origin has Bezos' financial backing

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.870
and he is one of the
wealthiest men in the world.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.360
Bezos has funneled as much
as roughly $1 billion a year

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.360
into the space venture to
fund his cosmic vision.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.930
- It's one Earth.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.400
I wanna go on this flight

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.830
because it's a thing I've
wanted to do all my life.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:45.080
It's an adventure.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.800
- [Narrator] Bezos is even stepping away

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.880
from his role as CEO of Amazon,

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.110
one of the largest companies in the world

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.164
in part to focus on Blue Origin.

00:04:53.164 --> 00:04:56.610
If Blue Origin's rocket
lifts off on schedule,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.400
it will be a big win for the company.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.350
- The important thing
about this first launch

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:04.150
is it'll be potentially the first time

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.470
that Blue Origin has
achieved something it said

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.780
it was gonna do that everyone can grasp.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.010
They've put people in space.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.920
- [Narrator] But
competitor Virgin Galactic

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.280
isn't far beyond.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.260
In August 2020, the company signaled plans

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.180
to launch its own tourism flights in 2021.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.980
So if it's a success,

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.380
the launch of New Shepard
would signal the start

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.490
of the next era of space tourism,

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.130
not only for Blue Origin

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.294
but for the larger space industry as well.

00:05:30.294 --> 00:05:33.044
(dramatic music)

